    ],
    "caption": "📡 Wi‑Fi 8 Targets Dropped Connections\n\nThe 802.11bn standard focuses on reliability rather than peak speed. It aims for 25% more throughput under weak signal, 25% fewer lost packets during access-point handoffs, and 25% lower latency at unstable moments.\n\nIts main mechanism is coordination between access points. Compatible devices can agree on who transmits and when, which should matter most for mesh systems struggling with walls and crowded apartment networks.\n\nThere is a catch. Wi‑Fi 8 routers already on sale use a draft specification, and the final version is expected only in 2028. The benefits require support from both the router and the client device, so Wi‑Fi 7 remains the practical choice for now.\n\n📊@tech",
